  "id": "belt-loaded-machine-glute-bridge",
  "name": "Belt-Loaded Machine Glute Bridge",
  "definition": "A bilateral belt-loaded compound bridge on the exact Life Fitness Insignia SS-GLB selectorized fixture. The hips press upward into a centered tightened belt, the feet stay on the footplate, the upper torso remains supported by the back pad, and the arms are crossed over the chest. The manufacturer endpoint aligns the femur with the spine. Hip extension follows the instruction; knee extension is a bounded categorical inference from visible opening in the start/finish diagrams, not measured kinematics or a transferred barbell knee angle. Gluteus maximus is primary and the vasti secondary by hip-dominant mechanics and existing action capabilities. Biceps femoris receives knee-control credit only because the visible region does not distinguish the long and short heads; it does not inherit hip-extension capability. Lumbar extensors, gluteus medius, and soleus provide conservative trunk, pelvic, and planted-foot control inferred from the fixture and anatomy. No deliberate spinal action, numerical joint range, barbell load equivalence, plate-loaded glute-drive topology, SS-GLD geometry, unilateral bridge, or band resistance is admitted.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "life-fitness-2024-insignia-series-manual",
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "christophy-2012-lumbar-spine"
  ],
